  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "1.10.51 — offline writes never wrote anything, and updating a missing row took the batch down.\n\n\t\t\t- AddAsync(row, isOffline: true) and UpdateAsync(row, isOffline: true) always failed with\n\t\t\t\"[Insert] StoreName can't be null\" and never touched the database. The offline write adds\n\t\t\tan OffLine flag to each row by turning it into an ExpandoObject, then handed the result\n\t\t\tback to the ordinary insert — where TModel is no longer your entity but object. Resolving\n\t\t\tthe store from it asked for one belonging to a model named \"Object\", got null, and the\n\t\t\tguard against a null store name refused the call. Nothing was ever wrong with the\n\t\t\tdatabase, so deleting and recreating it made no difference. The store name is now\n\t\t\tresolved once from the caller's TModel and passed down as a parameter. If you use the\n\t\t\toffline flag, this is the release that makes it work at all.\n\t\t\t- DbInsert and DbUpdate gained an overload taking the store name explicitly. The existing\n\t\t\tsignatures resolve it and delegate, so no caller changes.\n\t\t\t- Update on a row that does not exist no longer aborts the whole transaction. It read the\n\t\t\tstored row first to keep the columns the caller left out, and from undefined that merge\n\t\t\tproduced an empty object: put({}) then threw DataError because the key path yields no\n\t\t\tvalue, and it threw from inside the request handler where the surrounding try cannot see\n\t\t\tit — which in IndexedDB rolls back every other row in the batch. put is an upsert, so a\n\t\t\trow that is not there yet now goes in as-is, the handler body is wrapped, and a rejected\n\t\t\trow calls preventDefault() so the failure stays that row's. Updating a row another part\n\t\t\tof the app had just deleted was enough to reach this.",
